---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import '../styles/prose.css';

// What leaves a reader's device, from this site and from the Android app, and
// nothing else. Every line here is a claim about code in this repository, so a
// change that sends something new somewhere new changes this page in the same
// pull request. The Android app links here, as the store requires of an app
// that reads the device's location.

const description =
    'What the Grid Point Code website and Android app send, to whom, and what they keep on '
    + 'your device. No accounts, no cookies, no analytics.';

const REPO = 'https://github.com/octopranav/Grid-Point-Code';
---

<Base title="Privacy" description={description}>
    <div class="page">
        <article class="document">
            <h1>Privacy</h1>
            <p class="lede">
                No accounts, no cookies, no analytics, no advertising. A code is arithmetic
                your device does on its own. What does leave your device is listed below,
                with who receives it, and nothing else does.
            </p>
            <p class="note">In effect from 24 September 2026.</p>

            <h2 id="website">The website</h2>

            <h3>Serving the pages</h3>
            <p>
                The site is hosted on GitHub Pages. GitHub receives each request for a page or
                a file, with your IP address, as any web host does, under{' '}
                <a href="https://docs.github.com/en/site-policy/privacy-policies/github-general-privacy-statement" rel="noreferrer">GitHub's privacy statement</a>.
                The typefaces are served from this site, not from a font service.
            </p>

            <h3>Finding your country</h3>
            <p>
                The front page opens on a place in your country. To know which, it asks{' '}
                <code>api.country.is</code>, or <code>get.geojs.io</code> if the first does not
                answer in time, and each receives your IP address. It asks at most once a week,
                and the answer, a two-letter country code, is kept in your browser. If both are
                blocked, the page guesses from your device's time zone and language, which never
                leave the browser.
            </p>

            <h3>The map</h3>
            <p>
                Where a page shows a map, its tiles come from OpenFreeMap
                (<code>tiles.openfreemap.org</code>), which receives your IP address and the
                tiles asked for, and so the area on your screen.
            </p>

            <h3>Codes, names and landmarks</h3>
            <p>
                Encoding, decoding and correcting a code happen in your browser and send nothing.
                Searching by a place's name reads parts of an index from this site, so the host
                sees which part, and from that roughly what was typed. Anchoring a short form to a
                landmark reads the file of landmarks for the area around the place, about 200 km
                across, from this site.
            </p>

            <h3>Kept in your browser</h3>
            <p>
                Three settings: the theme, the basemap and the country above. For working
                offline, the pages, the landmark files already read or kept, and the map tiles
                already seen. Nothing is kept anywhere else, and clearing this site's data in your
                browser removes all of it.
            </p>

            <h2 id="app">The Android app</h2>

            <p>
                No account, no analytics, no crash reports, no advertising identifier, and no
                other company's code that reports anything.
            </p>

            <h3>Your location</h3>
            <p>
                Read only when you press the button to find where you are, or open the emergency
                card, and only while the app is open, never in the background. It comes from your
                device's own location service, and the app sends it nowhere: the code is worked out
                on the device.
            </p>

            <h3>What the app sends, and to whom</h3>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <strong>OpenFreeMap</strong> (<code>tiles.openfreemap.org</code>) receives
                    your IP address and the map tiles asked for, and so the area on your screen.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>This site</strong> (<code>gridpointcode.com</code>, on GitHub Pages)
                    receives your IP address and, when you search by a place's name, which part of
                    the name index was read; when you look up landmarks, which area's file, about
                    200 km across.
                </li>
            </ul>
            <p>
                Nothing else is sent. A code, a pair of coordinates or a location written in
                another system is read on the device, however it arrived.
            </p>

            <h3>Kept on your device</h3>
            <p>
                Your saved places, with their names and directions, and two settings, your basemap
                and the listener's language for reading aloud, are kept in the app's own storage.
                Android's device backup includes them if you have backup turned on, so a new phone
                has them. The landmark areas you keep for use offline, and the map tiles and
                landmarks already seen, stay on the device and are left out of backups.
                Uninstalling the app removes all of it.
            </p>

            <h3>Sharing and reading aloud</h3>
            <p>
                A code leaves the app only when you press Share or Copy, and only to the app you
                choose. Reading aloud uses your device's speech engine. Some engines send the text
                to their provider to make the voice; that is up to the engine you use and its own
                policy.
            </p>

            <h2 id="changes">Changes and questions</h2>
            <p>
                This page is kept in the same repository as the code it describes, so every
                change to it is in{' '}
                <a href={`${REPO}/commits/main/web/src/pages/privacy.astro`} rel="noreferrer">its history</a>.
                Questions about it can be asked by{' '}
                <a href={`${REPO}/issues`} rel="noreferrer">opening an issue</a>.
            </p>
        </article>
    </div>
</Base>

<style>
    /* The shared frame, as every other page has it. */
    .page {
        max-width: var(--shell);
        margin: 0 auto;
        padding: var(--space-5) var(--space-4);
    }

    .lede {
        font-size: var(--text-lede-size, 1.15rem);
    }

    .note {
        color: var(--ink-mid);
        font-size: var(--text-small-size);
    }

    @media (max-width: 600px) {
        .page {
            padding-inline: var(--space-3);
        }
    }
</style>
